  "$comment": "Templates are ATTRIBUTE-shaped, never geography-shaped. Nothing here says 'country' — they ask about an entity's attribute. That is what lets a wildlife or astronomy pack reuse them unchanged in v3.0, and it is the single most important property in this file.\n\nKNOWN GAP — tpl.location-of.mc4 builds for 35 of 65 countries. Its distractors are `same-region`, so the wrong answers are other SUBREGIONS of the same region — and a region with fewer than four subregions cannot fill four options. Europe and Africa have four each and work; Asia has three, North and South America and Oceania have one apiece, so their 30 countries produce no question. The fix is CONTENT, not code: author subregions, not more countries. Adding a seventh North American entity changes nothing, because all seven share one value — which is why `pnpm content:stats` saying 'add entities here first' is misleading for this template and right for every other one. Consequence to know about: tpl.country-to-map.mc4 builds for all 65, and it is the template a SIGHTED user gets. Its screen-reader sibling is this one. So for those 30 countries a screen-reader user gets no location question at all while a sighted user does. They lose nothing they had — capital, flag and currency questions are unaffected — but the location attribute is not yet equally askable by ear, and docs/design/accessibility.md is explicit that an equivalent must never be a lesser one. That is a gap to close, not a trade-off that has been accepted.",
